---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  type="article"
  title="Why the monitor runs off my hardware"
  description="How this site is monitored from the outside, why a monitor needs to survive the system it watches, and what happens when a check fails."
>
  <article class="post">
    <header>
      <p class="kind">Article · published 9 October 2026</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Why the monitor runs off my hardware</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A monitor that runs on the same machine it watches is useless when that machine fails.
        When the server goes down, the monitor goes down with it, and nobody gets an alert.
        This is why the system that watches this site runs somewhere else entirely.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="the-problem">
      <h2 id="the-problem">The problem with internal monitors</h2>
      <p>
        In September 2026, the old site's Azure VM was deleted with no backup and the site went offline.
        Because the entire setup lived on that one machine, anything that could have told me the site was down died at the exact same moment.
        You can read the full context in the <a href="/writing/september-2026-outage/">post-mortem for the September 2026 outage</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If I had installed a monitoring tool on that server, it would have been deleted along with the site.
        A monitor only works if it survives the incident it reports.
        If a server loses power, loses its network connection, or gets deleted, a monitor living on that server cannot send an alert.
        To know if a system is accessible to the outside world, you have to look at it from the outside world.
        That means the monitor must run off my hardware.
        Outages get noticed and recorded even when the homelab is switched off.
        A monitor on the machine it watches can't do that.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="what-runs-now">
      <h2 id="what-runs-now">What runs now</h2>
      <p>
        Monitoring is Upptime running on GitHub Actions.
        It runs on GitHub, not my hardware.
        This physical separation ensures that the monitoring stays alive regardless of what happens to the site or my local machines.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every five minutes a scheduled job requests three pages: Home, www, Projects.
        Because the job runs on shared runners, GitHub may delay the schedule based on their own system load.
        The interval is not always exactly five minutes, but it is frequent enough to catch any downtime.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="when-a-check-fails">
      <h2 id="when-a-check-fails">What happens when a check fails</h2>
      <p>
        The system records each result every time it checks those three pages.
        As long as the pages load correctly, the system logs the success and waits for the next cycle.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a check fails it opens a GitHub issue, which emails me.
        I receive an alert from a system that is still running, telling me about a system that is not.
      </p>
      <p>
        The monitor continues checking every five minutes while the site is down.
        When the site recovers the issue closes.
        This creates a clean, permanent record of exactly when the outage started and ended.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="what-it-does-not-cover">
      <h2 id="what-it-does-not-cover">What it does not cover yet</h2>
      <p>
        Right now, this external setup only watches the public site.
        It makes sure the pages that anyone on the internet can visit are actually loading.
      </p>
      <p>
        It does not cover the services running inside the homelab that are not exposed to the public internet.
        For those private services, an external monitor would not be able to reach them anyway.
        The plan for internal monitoring is to run Uptime Kuma inside the homelab for internal services.
        That tool will watch the internal systems from within the network, running alongside the external checks that already watch the public site.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="status-page">
      <h2 id="status-page">The public status page</h2>
      <p>
        Every test result, every open issue, and the overall uptime percentage are published automatically.
        Results publish to a public status page at <a href="https://status.bvrinfra.in">status.bvrinfra.in</a>.
        This page serves as the official record of the site reliability.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .post { max-width: 46rem; }
  .post header { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .kind { color: var(--muted); font-stretch: 85%; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .post section { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .post h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 1.1rem + 0.8vw, 1.75rem);
    font-stretch: 92%;
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }
  .post p { line-height: 1.65; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .post p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
</style>